$DIR/main.p:44:10: x holds a view of b (b, line 42), which was passed to reset(&b), which may free what it looks at or move it out (*p = ..., line 31) at line 43, so x can no longer be used here
    x looks at memory that b owns, without owning it: the view was made by b at
    line 42. A view of an owner held in a local variable may be used only while
    the variable keeps the value the view was made from. A call that may free
    memory the view looks at, or move an owner out of it, through a pointer to
    the variable or to memory it owns ends the view as well. At line 43 b was
    passed to reset(&b), which may free what it looks at or move it out (*p =
    ..., line 31), so the view may look at memory that was released, or at a
    value that now belongs to something else.
    Go keeps memory alive for as long as any pointer refers to it. Plutonium has
    no garbage collector: an owner releases its memory when it is dropped, so a
    view must not be used after the value it looks at has gone.
    Use x before line 43, or make that call only after the last use of x
    (see "Views" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.3)
    (run "pluto explain E3313" for a longer explanation)

